Package: rpi-headtracker
Architecture: arm64
Depends: adduser, kmod, ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: head tracker for sim racing that works as a USB joystick
 Turns a Raspberry Pi with a camera into a head tracker: the camera follows
 the driver's head and the Pi sends the head's turn and tilt to the PC as a
 USB joystick. Sims bind it like any game controller; no driver or software
 is needed on the PC.
 .
 Settings, a live view and help are on a web page (port 18080). Two face
 models: YuNet (OpenCV) and NeuralNet (the opentrack NeuralNet models, run
 with a private copy of ONNX Runtime).
 .
 Needs a Pi whose USB-C port can work as a device (Pi 4, Pi 5, Zero 2 W,
 CM4) and a 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS. The package turns on device mode for that
 port in config.txt; it takes effect after a restart.
 .
 For the Wi-Fi setup without a terminal, install rpi-headtracker-wifi too.

Package: rpi-headtracker-wifi
Architecture: all
Depends: dnsmasq-base,
         network-manager,
         polkitd,
         rpi-headtracker (>= ${source:Version}),
         systemd,
         ${misc:Depends}
Description: Wi-Fi setup for the RPi head tracker, without a terminal
 When the head tracker's Pi is on no Wi-Fi network (the first start, or the
 known network is gone for 90 seconds), it opens its own open Wi-Fi
 "HeadTracker-XXXX". A phone or PC that joins it gets the tracker's setup page
 by itself (captive portal), picks the home network and types its password.
 .
 Contains the service that opens the hotspot, port 80 on the hotspot for the
 setup page, a DNS setting that points every name to the tracker while the
 hotspot is open, and the permission for the tracker (user "headtracker") to
 change the Wi-Fi through NetworkManager.
 .
 Careful: on a Pi that is reached over its Wi-Fi, a lost network makes it open
 the hotspot instead; it is then reachable only there (or over Ethernet).
